cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below. Everything it computes is SYNTHETIC, classical software simulation with UNCALIBRATED placeholder thresholds; it is not field evidence and makes no superiority claims. Cite release v0.1.0 (the Zenodo DOI is added once the archive exists)."
title: "spark-membrane: a fail-closed console that orchestrates pinned OES repositories without merging them"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/spark-membrane"
url: "https://sparkainlp-x.github.io/spark-membrane/"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-10-05"
abstract: >-
  Offline, standard-library Python console that audits SYNTHETIC native
  32-channel frames with a conjunction of separate deterministic checks
  taken from pinned repositories: a protocol hash, the normative OES-32
  residual, the oes32_engine Profile A sidecar and the OES-Resilience
  weighted score. The two OES-32 formulas are never blended. It reports
  the failing engine and channel index, and engine disagreement with
  advisory max-abs, EWMA and CUSUM baselines (calibrated on a separate
  event-free stream, restarted after alarms). One new component, a seeded
  protocol-capped one-index explorer, reports REPAIRED_IN_SIM or
  LATCH_HELD in simulation only. Runs are hash-chained and rendered as an
  evidence passport, and a claims gate refuses overclaims. An optional
  extra verifies Ed25519 capabilities. All thresholds are uncalibrated
  placeholders; the project leads with the negative results of the
  repositories it builds on.
keywords:
  - fail-closed
  - audit
  - telemetry
  - synthetic data
  - reproducibility
  - evidence
  - research software
  - python
  - OES32
